# RFC8621 4.1.4: textBody and htmlBody are both "the body",
# chosen by what the client prefers. A message with only HTML has
# it in both lists, and its preview is that HTML with the markup
# taken out. An alternative gives each list the form it wants.
depends imap/empty-inbox
send 9
a append inbox () {323+}
From: shop@example.com
To: script@test.oryx.com
Subject: your receipt
Message-Id: <html1@example.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0100
Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8

<html><head><style>p{color:red}</style></head><body><p>Thank you &amp; farewell, <b>Gøril</b>!</p><p>Total: 42&nbsp;kr</p></body></html>
receive 4
* 1 EXISTS
* 1 RECENT
* OK [UIDNEXT 2] next uid
a OK [APPENDUID 1 1] done
send 17
b append inbox () {370+}
From: shop@example.com
To: script@test.oryx.com
Subject: both forms
Message-Id: <alt1@example.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 10:01:00 +0100
Content-Type: multipart/alternative; boundary="x"

--x
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

Plain words for plain clients.
--x
Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8

<p>Rich <i>words</i> for rich clients.</p>
--x--
receive 4
* 2 EXISTS
* 2 RECENT
* OK [UIDNEXT 3] next uid
b OK [APPENDUID 1 2] done
protocol http api
# the HTML-only message: both lists name the one part, and the preview is readable text
